#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c12ac46-65be-560d-9f11-fe9d7449b413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266818} {:answer/id #uuid "5d615371-d30a-5061-a8d8-53c4cddd2cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=2y+\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266968} {:answer/id #uuid "69b370cf-2c77-55a6-8fef-0dc70f82194d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=2y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267080} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d90385-1c8d-507f-a431-c96e74e25c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268226} {:answer/id #uuid "f9acbcf2-7dff-58f1-a11d-910c512a2b90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c12ac46-65be-560d-9f11-fe9d7449b413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266818} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e7d076-c660-5037-a4ff-af8a315c239c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22a41e65-3d40-52de-a0eb-a4c7353c0c15" :question/math-academy-id "q-132593" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z-0 & =4(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z & =4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050297e0-14ea-5f6a-aba2-48bd43bf28c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{2}y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266190} {:answer/id #uuid "22f28163-088c-5d95-9a9a-2af096df3146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=\\frac{3}{2}y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266462} {:answer/id #uuid "33de049e-5b2f-5cc1-9723-2daf22363b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{4}y-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266600} {:answer/id #uuid "7a114e7f-482b-5446-b9af-8ac3b7d1f2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267221} {:answer/id #uuid "d93410da-a216-5a9b-90e2-fe7a6af727ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d93410da-a216-5a9b-90e2-fe7a6af727ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268070} :answer-field/id #uuid "92a71cca-98d2-5245-a4d1-fd31406052e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac300975-45c9-5a2c-a8b5-5d2addf48a83" :question/math-academy-id "q-132595" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Given that the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-\\frac{2{x}^{2}+y}{{y}^{3}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(\\sqrt{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},2) & =-\\frac{2(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4+2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{3}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72b4af52-0cb5-5170-b305-ddbac150686d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267149} {:answer/id #uuid "a7393b59-39d3-5aad-8cbc-5f6ab101035b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267660} {:answer/id #uuid "bc72c95f-ce36-52f6-88e0-ea6a281e11ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267831} {:answer/id #uuid "c452d620-354c-5b6f-b5ca-b7e2e4c22b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267897} {:answer/id #uuid "dfecef19-f61e-5834-b586-b5f0a09dbb4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfecef19-f61e-5834-b586-b5f0a09dbb4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268121} :answer-field/id #uuid "b75921e8-74b4-5a5e-9fcb-280d1ebead77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ecd11e65-1ebe-5f6c-b826-3c8a8ba99741" :question/math-academy-id "q-132602" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,7)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+{1}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =0+1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-7 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,7)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+7$ (or $x-z+7=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d882919-8d13-50f7-840d-8a1f45fcd980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3i+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333253} {:answer/id #uuid "543ab52d-1623-54f6-b166-c0e43d208801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333756} {:answer/id #uuid "a803a6f4-b31b-5b47-92f1-472e09aa720a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334459} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0b4881-8db8-5a4e-a73c-944bf83e8664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335177} {:answer/id #uuid "fa97ca9a-10f2-55a2-b950-5d1537242daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa0b4881-8db8-5a4e-a73c-944bf83e8664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335177} :answer-field/id #uuid "234510dd-0314-5bd3-949a-5dd7a7a7bea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "725a1fb6-c62e-5bb7-9fe8-49c03c5250ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-64971" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2(x+y)-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1+1)-2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,1) & ={f}_{x}(1,1)(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2x-2 \\\\ z & =2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2x-1$ (or $2x-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-2\\,k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-2\\,k)=i+2\\,k$ is also parallel to it."}]}
